# .worktreeinclude - files copied into new `claude --worktree` checkouts.
# https://code.claude.com/docs/en/worktrees
#
# Uses .gitignore syntax. Only files that BOTH match a pattern here AND are
# git-ignored are copied, so tracked files (e.g. .dev.vars.example) are never
# duplicated. This lets a fresh worktree run `make dev` without hand-copying
# local Cloudflare Workers secrets.
#
# ── Accepted trade-off: this copies REAL secrets, and not only for you ────────
# These patterns apply to every worktree Claude Code creates with git - which
# includes SUBAGENT worktrees (`isolation: worktree`) and desktop parallel
# sessions, not just your own `claude --worktree`. So a subagent's throwaway
# checkout gets a real copy of every .dev.vars / .env on disk.
#
# What keeps that safe is the `Read`/`Edit` deny rules in the repository root's
# .claude/settings.json, which are only present inside a worktree if `.claude`
# is checked out there. Sparse checkout writes root-level FILES but NOT
# root-level DIRECTORIES, so `worktree.sparsePaths` in .claude/settings.json
# MUST keep ".claude" in its list (alongside "make" and "hooks", which `make ci`
# and the PreToolUse guards need). Shortening that list silently strips the deny
# rules from every worktree and leaves these secrets in it unprotected.
#
# Do not "fix" this by deleting the patterns below: that breaks `make dev` in a
# worktree, which is the entire reason this file exists.

# Cloudflare Workers local secrets
**/.dev.vars
**/.dev.vars.*

# Global environment files
**/.env
**/.env.*
